<header><p class="st-kicker">theocracy · North America and Oceania</p><h1 class="st-title">State of Deseret</h1><p class="st-meta">1849 CE – 1851 CE · capital Salt Lake City</p></header>
<p class="st-teaser">A vast state proposed by Mormon settlers in 1849, governed in practice by the Latter-day Saint leadership under Brigham Young until Congress created Utah Territory.</p>
<div class="st-body"><div class="st-text"><h2>Origins</h2><p>Driven from <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/illinois">Illinois</a>, members of the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/church-of-jesus-christ-of-latter-day-saints">Church of Jesus Christ of Latter-day Saints</a> reached the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/salt-lake-valley">Salt Lake Valley</a> in 1847, then in Mexican territory. After the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/treaty-of-guadalupe-hidalgo">Treaty of Guadalupe Hidalgo</a> made the region American, church leaders drafted a constitution in 1849 for a State of Deseret, a name from the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/book-of-mormon">Book of Mormon</a> meaning honeybee, and petitioned Congress for admission.</p><h2>Rule and society</h2><p>Deseret claimed a huge area covering most of present-day <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/nevada">Nevada</a>, much of <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/arizona">Arizona</a> and parts of six other states, with a corridor to the Pacific at <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/san-diego">San Diego</a>. <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/brigham-young">Brigham Young</a> was elected governor, and church officers filled the legislature and courts, so civil and religious authority were effectively one. The government organised irrigation, roads, a militia and the <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/university-of-utah">University of Deseret</a>, the future University of Utah.</p><h2>Decline and legacy</h2><p>Congress rejected statehood and in the Compromise of 1850 created the much smaller <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah-territory">Utah Territory</a>, with Young as governor. The Deseret assembly dissolved itself in April 1851. A shadow legislature met for years afterwards, and <a class="enc-link" href="/encyclopedia/utah">Utah</a> became a state in 1896.</p></div><aside class="st-side"><figure class="st-fig"><img src="/images/states/state-of-deseret.jpg" alt="State of Deseret" width="320" height="193"><figcaption>Image: Unknown author · Public domain · <a href="https://commons.wikimedia.org/wiki/File:Mormon_Flag_of_God%C2%B4s_Kingdom_(2002).svg" target="_blank" rel="noopener">source</a></figcaption></figure><dl class="st-facts"><dt>Years</dt><dd>1849 CE – 1851 CE</dd><dt>Century</dt><dd><a href="/centuries/19th-century">19th century</a></dd><dt>Type</dt><dd>theocracy</dd><dt>Character</dt><dd>theocratic</dd><dt>Capital</dt><dd>Salt Lake City</dd><dt>Rulers</dt><dd>Brigham Young</dd><dt>Religion</dt><dd>Latter-day Saint movement</dd><dt>Language</dt><dd>English</dd><dt>Today</dt><dd><a href="/countries/united-states">United States</a></dd><dt>Also</dt><dd>Deseret</dd></dl><figure class="st-locator"><a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=state-of-deseret" aria-label="State of Deseret on the atlas"><svg class="map map-loc" viewBox="1939 722 640 512" role="img" aria-label="Map: where State of Deseret lay"><image href="/maps/world.svg" x="0" y="0" width="10000" height="4393"/><path class="map-tie" d="M2259 978L2570 1008"/><path class="map-dot-n" d="M2570 1008h0"/><path class="map-dot-halo" d="M2259 978h0"/><path class="map-dot-me" d="M2259 978h0"/></svg></a><figcaption>The dot marks Salt Lake City. <a href="/atlas?year=1850&amp;focus=state-of-deseret">On the atlas &rarr;</a></figcaption></figure></aside></div>
